l,1.001671171015136,null,1.0562482046056405,null,1.093715809338311,null,1.106641447931164,null,1.093721130919728,null,1.0596751811121385,null,1.0137885870416938,null,0.9675032113886355,null,0.9316599630799167,null,0.9140361527351346,null,0.9177227833059951,null,0.9406679063891827,null,0.976430150115078,null,1.0159078254609002,null,1.0495968011940586,null,1.069830024301861],"role":"reference"}],"worst":4.440892098500626e-16,"worst_label":"largest |difference|","note":"This row checks the reference, not PyStratify: the Sommerfeld integrals written without PyStratify (emitter_references.py), given a perfect mirror (r_s = −1, r_p = 1), must give the image dipole's closed form, Drexhage's oscillations; a magnetic dipole sees the mirror with the opposite sign. The same integrals are then PyStratify's reference for real stacks (the Sommerfeld row, 1e-10).","script":"benchmarks/pages/films_dipole_consistency.py","function":"plot_perfect_mirror","rmse":1.5536512468907993e-16,"summary":"the Sommerfeld reference itself against the image dipole of a perfect mirror","aside":"Given a perfect mirror, $r_s = -1$ and $r_p = 1$, the Sommerfeld integrals must reproduce the image dipole (Drexhage; Chance, Prock & Silbey), with $x = kd = 4\\pi h/\\lambda$:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Gamma_\\perp}{\\Gamma_0} = 1 + 3\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x^3} - \\frac{\\cos x}{x^2}\\right)$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\Gamma_\\parallel}{\\Gamma_0} = 1 - \\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{x^2} - \\frac{\\sin x}{x^3}\\right)$$\n\nA magnetic dipole sees the mirror with the opposite sign, $2 - \\Gamma_\\parallel/\\Gamma_0$. This checks the reference every other film-emitter row relies on."}
